You are checking whether this repository's documentation still describes its code.

Two files are in your working directory:

- `recent-commits.txt` — the last 200 commit subjects.
- `documents.txt` — every Markdown file tracked in the repository.

Read the documents that the recent commits touched the code behind, and report only
where a document states something the code contradicts: a command that no longer
exists, a flag that was renamed, a default that moved, a field a document says is
accepted and the code refuses.

What would make this answer wrong:

- Reporting a document as stale without having read the code it describes. A commit
  subject is not evidence about a document; the file the document names is.
- Reporting prose you would have written differently. Out of date is a fact about the
  code; badly worded is an opinion, and nobody acts on it.
- Reporting the same drift twice under two titles. One finding per document.
- Inventing a file path. Every path you name must appear in `documents.txt`.

Answer with JSON only: {"findings": [{"title": ..., "body": ...}]}. `title` names the
document and the drift in one line. `body` quotes what the document says, names the
file and symbol that contradicts it, and says what would make it true again.

An empty `findings` array is the expected answer, and it is a better one than a
finding you are not sure about.
